2\({}^{\circ}\)) Let \(\kappa\) be a tetrahedron in \(\mathbb{R}^{3}\) with faces denoted by \(f\) and let \(\textbf{u}\!\in\!H^{1}(\kappa)^{3}\). We define the two sets of moments of **u** on \(\kappa\) by:

\[N_{f}(\textbf{u})=\int_{f}\textbf{u}\!\cdot\!\textbf{n}\,q\,ds\quad\quad\forall q \!\in\!P_{l-1}(f),\]

\[N_{\kappa}(\textbf{u})=\int_{\kappa}\textbf{u}\!\cdot\!\textbf{q}\,dx\quad \quad\forall\textbf{q}\!\in\!P_{l-2}^{3}(\kappa).\]

We can immediately check that for **u** in \(D_{l}\), \(\textbf{u}\!\cdot\!\textbf{n}\) belongs to \(P_{l-1}\) on each face \(f\) of \(\kappa\). In addition, it easy to see that when **u** is a _divergence-free_ vector field of \(D_{l}\) then **u** belongs to \(P_{l-1}^{3}\).

As usual, let \(\hat{\kappa}\) denote the unit reference tetrahedron. Instead of (5.26), let us transform vector functions defined on \(\kappa\) by the contravariant transformation:

\[\hat{\textbf{u}}=B_{\kappa}^{-1}(\textbf{u}\circ F_{\kappa})\quad\quad\forall \textbf{u}\text{ defined on }\kappa.\] (5.51)

It coincides with the contravariant transformation (4.63), up to the multiplicative factor \(J_{F}\) which is constant here:

\[J_{F}=|\det(B_{\kappa})|.\]

As a consequence (5.51) preserves entirely the divergence:

\[(\operatorname{div}\textbf{u})\circ F_{\kappa}=\operatorname{div}\hat{ \textbf{u}}.\] (5.52)

In addition, we have the analogue of Lemmas 5.5, 5.6 and Theorem 5.3.

**Proposition 5.2**.: \(1^{\circ}\)_) The space \(D_{l}\) is invariant under the transformation (5.51) and the moments of **u** given by Definition 5.3 vanish on \(\kappa\) iff the same moments of \(\hat{\textbf{u}}\) vanish on \(\hat{\kappa}\)._

\(2^{\circ}\)_) A vector field **u** of \(D_{l}\) is entirely determined in a tetrahedron \(\kappa\) by its two sets of moments: \(N_{f}(\textbf{u})\), \(N_{\kappa}(\textbf{u})\). Moreover, the normal component of **u** on a given face \(f\) of \(\kappa\) depends only upon the moments \(N_{f}(\textbf{u})\) defined on that face._

**Definition 5.4**.: Let \(\textbf{u}\!\in\!H^{1}(\kappa)^{3}\), where \(\kappa\) is an arbitrary tetrahedron. Its interpolant \(\omega_{\kappa}\textbf{u}\) is the unique polynomial of \(D_{l}\) that has the same moments as **u** on \(\kappa\).

Thus \(\omega_{\kappa}\textbf{u}\) is determined by the conditions:

\[N_{\kappa}(\textbf{u}-\omega_{\kappa}\textbf{u})=\{0\},\quad\quad N_{f}( \textbf{u}-\omega_{\kappa}\textbf{u})=\{0\}.\]

Again, the invariance in Proposition 5.2 implies that

\[\widehat{\omega_{\kappa}\textbf{u}}=\omega_{\hat{\kappa}}\hat{\textbf{u}}.\]

Moreover, we observe that

\[\operatorname{div}(\textbf{u}-\omega_{\kappa}\textbf{u})=0\quad\text{on }\kappa \quad\quad\forall\textbf{u}\!\in\!P_{l}^{3}\]

and that \(\operatorname{div}\textbf{u}=0\) on \(\kappa\) implies that \(\operatorname{div}(\omega_{\kappa}\textbf{u})=0\).
